  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let E be a type with a normed additive commutative group structure, a real inner product space structure, finite dimension over R, and a measurable space structure equal to its Borel structure. For every arbitrary function F:E→R and every point p∈E, and for every real scalar s with s≠0, the pushforward under A(x)=p+s x of volume.tilted(x↦-F(x)) equals volume.tilted(x↦-F(s^{-1}(x-p))):\nA_*(volume.tilted(-F)) = volume.tilted(x↦-F(s^{-1}(x-p))).\nReal-vector multiplication is scalar multiplication; volume is the finite real Hilbert Lebesgue/Haar measure. For any measure nu and real function f, nu.tilted(f) literally denotes nu.withDensity(x↦ENNReal.ofReal(exp(f(x))/(∫exp(f)dnu))). This Bochner integral normalization is totalized when exponential integrability fails; the equality is equality of those literal measures. The affine map is the genuine measurable pushforward map, and the right-hand potential is composed with its inverse x↦s^{-1}(x-p). There is no CompleteSpace binder, no nontriviality assumption, and no hypothesis that F is measurable, integrable, differentiable, convex, or otherwise regular. There is no probability-measure assumption or conclusion and no assertion of exponential integrability or a positive normalization. Negative s is allowed and E may have dimension zero. The nonzero scalar is the sole explicit hypothesis after F and p; its quantifier precedes the proof argument s≠0. No Gaussian, posterior family, moment bound, or gradient conclusion occurs.",
